import{f as s,J as d,o,c as h,k as t,a,a8 as l,a9 as m,a6 as i,b as c,w as n,K as g,a7 as f}from"./chunks/framework.Dd1qhYRL.js";const D=JSON.parse('{"title":"Why ignatius","description":"The case for modeling before building, who reads the model, why ignatius is a separate tool from noorm, and where the name comes from.","frontmatter":{"title":"Why ignatius","description":"The case for modeling before building, who reads the model, why ignatius is a separate tool from noorm, and where the name comes from."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"modeling/why.md","filePath":"modeling/why.md"}'),p={name:"modeling/why.md"};function u(w,e,b,y,v,k){const r=d("Mermaid");return o(),h("div",null,[e[1]||(e[1]=t("h1",{id:"why-ignatius",tabindex:"-1"},[a("Why ignatius "),t("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#why-ignatius","aria-label":"Permalink to “Why ignatius”"},"​")],-1)),e[2]||(e[2]=t("p",null,[a("Most schemas get designed while they are being built, so the shape of the whole lives in migration history and in the head of whoever wrote it. "),t("a",{href:"https://github.com/noormdev/ignatius",target:"_blank",rel:"noreferrer"},"ignatius"),a(" moves that design earlier. You describe entities, keys, and relationships in markdown, and it renders a diagram people can argue about before anyone writes SQL. It never connects to a database and never generates DDL.")],-1)),e[3]||(e[3]=t("div",{class:"demo-video"},[t("video",{src:m,poster:l,width:"1316",height:"858",controls:"",muted:"",playsinline:"",preload:"none"})],-1)),e[4]||(e[4]=i('<p>Above: a model of an LLM memory database in all three views.</p><h2 id="what-does-it-produce" tabindex="-1">What does it produce? <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-does-it-produce" aria-label="Permalink to “What does it produce?”">​</a></h2><p>One folder of markdown drives three views of the same model.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>View</th><th>Notation</th><th>Answers</th><th>Shows</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Data Graph</td><td>IDEF1X</td><td>What is the data, and how is it identified?</td><td>Entities in crow&#39;s-foot notation, identifying relationships, compound keys that carry their lineage, subtype clusters</td></tr><tr><td>Data Dictionary</td><td>none</td><td>What is each thing for?</td><td>Every entity, process, external, and store on one searchable page</td></tr><tr><td>Data Flows</td><td>SSADM data flow diagrams</td><td>Who touches the data, and where does it live?</td><td>Processes, external actors, and stores, where a store is any place data rests: a table, a cache, a log file, a queue, a paper form</td></tr></tbody></table><p>See <a href="/modeling/entities.html">Entities and key inheritance</a> and <a href="/modeling/data-flows.html">Data flows</a>.</p><p>You write keys and relationships as text, and ignatius derives cardinality, entity classification, and subtype clusters from the key shape. Change a foreign key and the notation updates on the next reload. The model diffs and reviews like code.</p><p>A table list says what exists. The graph also shows how entities connect, where identity comes from, and which clusters share a basetype. Each entity carries prose on why it exists, plus example rows, and a sample row that violates a constraint you believed in exposes a modeling error no structural check will catch.</p><h2 id="why-model-before-building" tabindex="-1">Why model before building? <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-model-before-building" aria-label="Permalink to “Why model before building?”">​</a></h2><p>The industry mostly skips modeling and goes straight to building. In my experience, modeling first solves about 80% of the problems up front and gives months of valid programming work before anyone opens an editor.</p><p>The other 20% is real: once you start building, you do find problems in the model. What matters is when. While nothing is committed to the system, changing the model costs an edit to a text file. Once it is code, it does not.</p><p>Review sits between writing the model and writing the schema, so a disagreement about the data surfaces while it is cheap:</p>',11)),(o(),c(f,null,{default:n(()=>[g(r,{id:"mermaid-100",class:"mermaid",graph:"flowchart%20TD%0A%20%20%20%20W%5B%22Write%20entity%20and%3Cbr%2F%3Eflow%20files%22%5D%20--%3E%20V%5B%22ignatius%20validate%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20V%20--%3E%20S%5B%22ignatius%20serve%3Cbr%2F%3Eor%20export%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20S%20--%3E%20D%7B%22Stakeholders%20agree%3F%22%7D%0A%20%20%20%20D%20--%3E%7Cno%7C%20W%0A%20%20%20%20D%20--%3E%7Cyes%7C%20B%5B%22Agent%20writes%20the%20schema%3Cbr%2F%3Efrom%20the%20same%20files%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20B%20--%3E%20N%5B%22noorm%20builds%20and%3Cbr%2F%3Eversions%20the%20SQL%22%5D%0A"})]),fallback:n(()=>[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a(" Loading... ",-1)])]),_:1})),e[5]||(e[5]=i('<h2 id="who-reads-the-model" tabindex="-1">Who reads the model? <a class="header-anchor" href="#who-reads-the-model" aria-label="Permalink to “Who reads the model?”">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Reader</th><th>Gets</th><th>How</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Stakeholder</td><td>A diagram to explore, a dictionary to search, and prose on business meaning</td><td><code>ignatius export</code> writes one self-contained HTML file that opens from <code>file://</code> with nothing installed</td></tr><tr><td>Coding agent</td><td>Plain markdown with YAML frontmatter to read and write</td><td>The <a href="/modeling/modeling-skill.html"><code>ignatius-modeling</code> skill</a> authors entity and flow files through guided Q&amp;A and checks each with <code>ignatius validate</code></td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="why-is-it-a-separate-tool-from-noorm" tabindex="-1">Why is it a separate tool from noorm? <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-is-it-a-separate-tool-from-noorm" aria-label="Permalink to “Why is it a separate tool from noorm?”">​</a></h2><p>noorm already explores schemas and runs SQL, so it is fair to ask why modeling is not a <code>noorm model</code> subcommand. Three reasons:</p><ul><li><strong>It never touches a database.</strong> ignatius carries no database driver and emits no DDL. It is useful before the database exists, when a tool that needs a connection has nothing to offer.</li><li><strong>It does not need noorm.</strong> Nothing in the format is specific to noorm: markdown in, IDEF1X and SSADM diagrams out, no dialect anywhere. A team on any other migration tool gets the same thing.</li><li><strong>The audiences differ.</strong> <code>ignatius export</code> produces a file for the person approving a design. That reader has no reason to install a schema manager.</li></ul><h2 id="where-does-the-name-come-from" tabindex="-1">Where does the name come from? <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-does-the-name-come-from" aria-label="Permalink to “Where does the name come from?”">​</a></h2><p>ignatius is named for Derek Ignatius Asirvadem, who mentored me for a couple of years and introduced me to IDEF1X. NIST published IDEF1X as FIPS 184 in 1993, and it became part of ISO/IEC/IEEE 31320-2 in 2012. Studying it and then practising it convinced me there is no higher standard for modeling data. The <a href="/modeling/">landing page</a> traces its lineage back to Codd in 1970.</p><p>Before ignatius I drew models in OmniGraffle, on Derek&#39;s recommendation. My own obsession with perfect lines and names turned drawing into the bottleneck, and a picture cannot tell an LLM how two entities relate. So the model moved into markdown, where the layout is generated and an agent can read and write the source.</p><p>None of these tools would exist without his guidance. ignatius is a tribute to him.</p><h2 id="next" tabindex="-1">Next <a class="header-anchor" href="#next" aria-label="Permalink to “Next”">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="/modeling/installation.html">ignatius commands</a>: install, serve, validate, export</li><li><a href="/modeling/entities.html">Entities and key inheritance</a></li><li><a href="/modeling/data-flows.html">Data flows</a></li><li><a href="/modeling/reverse-engineering.html">Reverse-engineering</a>: extract a model from a database you already run</li><li><a href="/modeling/best-practices.html">Best practices</a></li></ul>',11))])}const _=s(p,[["render",u]]);export{D as __pageData,_ as default};
